      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${u}^{4}-26{u}^{2}+25?$\n\nI. $u=1$\n\nII. $u=5$\n\nIII. $u=3$",
      "worked_solution": "- Substituting $1$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{4}-26(1)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 1-26(1)+25 & = \\\\ 1-26+25 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $1$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $1$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $5$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5)^{4}-26(5)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 625-26(25)+25 & = \\\\ 625-650+25 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $5$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $5$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $3$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{4}-26(3)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 81-26(9)+25 & = \\\\ 81-234+25 & = \\\\ -128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $3$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $3$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the roots are $u=1$ and $u=5,$ so the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
