    "q-37656": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37656",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x+1?$\n\nI. $x=-6$\n\nII. $x=0$\n\nIII. $x=2$",
      "worked_solution": "- Substituting $-6$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6)^{3}-6(-6)^{2}+9(-6)+1 & = \\\\ -216-216-54+1 & = \\\\ -485\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-6$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $-6$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $0$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{3}-6(0)^{2}+9(0)+1 & = \\\\ 0-0+0+1 & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $0$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $0$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{3}-6(2)^{2}+9(2)+1 & = \\\\ 8-24+18+1 & = \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $2$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, there are no roots among the given options, so the correct answer is \"None\".",
